From: Xun2202 <98207886+Xun2202@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:27:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] presentation: fast scroller grabs on the thumb or on a hold
 beside it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A 12 dp hit box on the drawn thumb is hard to hit, but the old 32 dp box
grabbed on down, so every quick swipe that started near the end edge
dragged the thumb: the list jumped by the scroll ratio and had no coast.
That was worst for the return flick at the end of the list, which starts
right on top of the bottom thumb.

The hit area is 32 dp plus the end inset again, in two parts:

- On the drawn thumb (plus the end inset) the thumb is grabbed on down,
  as before. The content never sees that pointer.
- Beside it, the other 20 dp toward the content shares pointer input with
  the content and consumes nothing. The thumb is grabbed only if the
  finger stays down and within touch slop for 120 ms while the content is
  not already scrolling. A flick crosses slop sooner and scrolls the
  content with its coast. A down during a coast stops the content and is
  never a grab. After a grab every change is consumed in the initial pass,
  the y movement drags the thumb, and DragInteraction start / stop marks
  it as dragged.

Overlapping siblings are not all hit-tested: hit testing stops at the
first sibling that is hit unless that node shares pointer input. The
part beside the thumb is a PointerInputModifierNode whose
sharePointerInputWithSiblings() is true. Without that the content gets
no events there. The KDoc that said otherwise is corrected.

120 ms is well below the 500 ms long press timeout. A moving finger
after the grab cancels an item long press. A finger held perfectly still
for 500 ms can still fire it.

The same applies to all four scrollers here, including the fourth one,
IrregularVerticalGridFastScroller.

