 /**
- * Drawn thumb stays [ThumbThickness] by [ThumbLength]. The hit rectangle is that same
- * width, plus [endInset] on the end edge.
+ * Drawn thumb stays [ThumbThickness] by [ThumbLength] on the end edge. The hit area is
+ * [ThumbTouchWidth] plus [endInset] wide and [ThumbLength] tall, in two parts:
  *
- * Harmony: the hit rectangle used to be 32 dp, grown toward the content. The thumb stays
- * visible for the fade delay after a fling, and [startDragImmediately] consumes the down.
- * A swipe that started in that strip dragged the thumb, jumped the list by the scroll
- * ratio, and had no coast. A down on the drawn thumb still drags immediately. A down
- * anywhere else, including during that coast, scrolls the content.
+ * - Thumb block: the drawn thumb plus [endInset]. A down here grabs the thumb at once
+ *   ([harmonyThumbTouch]); the content never sees the pointer.
+ * - Beside it: the remaining [ThumbTouchWidth] - [ThumbThickness] toward the content
+ *   ([harmonyHoldToGrab]). The content gets every event here. The thumb is grabbed only
+ *   when the finger stays down and within touch slop for [HoldToGrabMillis] while the
+ *   content is not already scrolling.
+ *
+ * A thumb-sized target is hard to hit, but a wide target that grabs on down turns every
+ * quick swipe near the edge into a thumb drag: the list jumps by the scroll ratio and has
+ * no coast. That is worst at the end of the list, where the thumb sits under the thumb
+ * of the hand that flicks back. A flick crosses touch slop well inside [HoldToGrabMillis],
+ * so beside the thumb a flick scrolls and a hold grabs. A down during a coast stops the
+ * content and is never a grab. [HoldToGrabMillis] is well below the 500 ms long press
+ * timeout, so a hold reaches the thumb before an item long press would fire.
